# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# CLIENT CONFIG — TEMPLATE ONLY. This file is COMMITTED; it documents which
# variables a build may supply, never their real values.
#
# Vite only exposes variables prefixed VITE_, and it INLINES them at BUILD time.
# They are NOT read when the page loads, so a production value must be present
# when `npm run build` runs — setting it as a runtime config var does nothing.
#
#   local dev/demo → leave VITE_API_URL UNSET (see below)
#   production     → set VITE_API_URL in the build environment, then build
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Where the CoDecep server is. Used by both the HTTP client (src/lib/api.js)
# and the Socket.io connection (src/socket.js) — they talk to the same server.
#
# LEAVE IT UNSET for BOTH normal cases. src/lib/config.js picks the right
# default from the build mode:
#
#   dev build  (npm run dev)   → http://localhost:3001, exactly what the client
#                                used to hardcode. Local dev needs no env file.
#   prod build (npm run build) → "" (EMPTY = SAME ORIGIN). This is the
#                                single-app Heroku deploy: one dyno serves this
#                                bundle AND the API, so calls go to "/api/..."
#                                relative to whatever host served the page.
#                                Nothing to configure and nothing to get wrong.
#
# Set it ONLY for a TWO-HOST deploy (static client on one origin, API on
# another), to the API's origin, with no trailing path (a trailing slash is
# stripped for you). Then the SERVER's CORS_ORIGIN must name this client's
# origin, or the browser will block the calls:
#   VITE_API_URL=https://your-api-host.example.com
# VITE_API_URL=

# Per-request client debug traces (src/debug.js). Leave unset/off normally.
# VITE_DEBUG=true
